Case Study 135: Project TerraFusion –
Global Climate Engineering and Environmental Control Platform

Business Explanation:
TerraFusion Environmental Systems operates an advanced climate engineering platform
responsible for monitoring and coordinating large-scale environmental initiatives including
carbon capture facilities, renewable energy networks, atmospheric monitoring systems, climate
simulation engines, water resource infrastructure, and ecological restoration programs.
Governments, scientific institutions, environmental agencies, and international organizations
rely on the platform to manage complex environmental interventions and assess their long-term
impact.

The organization processes petabytes of environmental data generated by satellites, weather
systems, IoT sensor networks, oceanographic monitoring stations, AI-powered climate models,
and research facilities. Existing infrastructure struggles to support increasing computational
workloads, deployment complexity, infrastructure scalability, and operational visibility
requirements. Several environmental simulation exercises exposed weaknesses in observability,
disaster recovery, infrastructure automation, and operational resilience. International
organizations require strict service availability, rapid recovery capabilities, and comprehensive
auditability due to the strategic importance of the platform.

Executive leadership has approved a cloud-native modernization initiative aimed at building a
globally distributed environmental operations ecosystem. Infrastructure must be provisioned
using Terraform, applications containerized through Docker, deployments orchestrated through
Kubernetes, and software delivery automated using Jenkins. Monitoring and observability must
be implemented using Prometheus, Grafana, ELK Stack, and Vault. During final evaluation,
reviewers may simulate cloud-region failures, climate model outages, cyberattacks, satellite
communication disruptions, failed deployments, and extreme environmental emergency
scenarios. The platform must demonstrate automated recovery, operational continuity,
self-healing infrastructure, and resilience under large-scale disruptions.

Objective: 
The objective of this project is to design, implement, and demonstrate an
enterprise-scale DevOps ecosystem supporting environmental intelligence operations,
infrastructure automation, operational monitoring, security, and disaster recovery.

Expected Outcome and Deliverables: 
Students are expected to deliver Dockerized services,
Terraform infrastructure automation, Kubernetes deployment manifests, Jenkins CI/CD
pipelines, monitoring dashboards, centralized logging systems, Vault integration, disaster
recovery procedures, security documentation, and a comprehensive architecture report
demonstrating scalability, resilience, operational excellence, and long-term sustainability.


